207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,1372,315,11457,88561,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,315,6785,1931,5109,1741,429,198,59,26056,2359,7,856,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,88,92,1124,1291,8,1124,2359,7,379,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,89,92,1124,1291,8,1124,2359,7,1147,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,87,92,1124,1291,8,284,220,23,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the number of ordered triples $(x,y,z)$ of positive real numbers such that\n\\[\\left( x + \\frac{1}{y} \\right) \\left( y + \\frac{1}{z} \\right) \\left( z + \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 8.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4d0c54f08f9a92886f79a62faed424855e9a324358ed0f9a0b7485ca7ce9c9c9"},"seconds":15.794224500656128,"seed":552741039513654349,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by analyzing the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\left( x + \\frac{1}{y} \\right) \\left( y + \\frac{1}{z} \\right) \\left( z + \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 8.\n\\]\n\nWe will use the AM-GM inequality to find a possible value for \\(x, y, z\\). The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\na + b \\geq 2\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\n\nApplying AM-GM to each term in the product, we get:\n\n\\[\nx + \\frac{1}{y} \\geq 2\\sqrt{x \\cdot \\frac{1}{y}} = 2\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\n\\]\n\\[\ny + \\frac{1}{z} \\geq 2\\sqrt{y \\cdot \\frac{1}{z}} = 2\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{z}},\n\\]\n\\[\nz + \\frac{1}{x} \\geq 2\\sqrt{z \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}} = 2\\sqrt{\\frac{z}{x}}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying these inequalities together, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left( x + \\frac{1}{y} \\right) \\left( y + \\frac{1}{z} \\right) \\left( z + \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\geq 8\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{y}{z} \\cdot \\frac{z}{x}} = 8\\sqrt{1} = 8.\n\\]\n\nThe equality holds in the AM-GM inequality if and only if \\(x = \\frac{1}{y}\\), \\(y = \\frac{1}{z}\\), and \\(z = \\frac{1}{x}\\). This implies \\(x = \\frac{1}{x}\\), \\(y = \\frac{1}{y}\\), and \\(z = \\frac{1}{z}\\), which means \\(x = y = z = 1\\). Substituting \\(x = y = z = 1\\) into the original equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{1} \\right) \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{1} \\right) \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{1} \\right) = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n\\]\n\nwhich satisfies the equation. Therefore, the only solution is \\(x = y = z = 1\\), and there is only one ordered triple \\((1, 1, 1)\\) that satisfies the equation.\n\nThus, the number of ordered triples \\((x, y, z)\\) of positive real numbers that satisfy the given equation is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:278"}
